# InnerSun environment variables.
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values. NEVER commit `.env`.
# For V1 everything runs on localhost.

# ---- API server ----
NODE_ENV=development
API_HOST=127.0.0.1
API_PORT=3001

# PORT is NOT set here — it is injected by hosting platforms, and the API treats its
# presence as the signal that it is deployed rather than local. Setting it changes three
# things at once: the service binds 0.0.0.0 instead of loopback, it trusts X-Forwarded-*
# headers so rate limiting keys on the real client rather than the proxy, and it stops
# reading this .env file entirely so that platform-provided configuration cannot be
# overridden by a stray file. Use API_PORT to change the port locally.
# Origin allowed to call the API (the web app during local dev)
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# Whether this instance serves POST /chat. On by default. Set to false on the hosted
# admin instance (Feature 17), whose only job is letting researchers author Care Patterns
# — an open, unauthenticated, token-spending chat endpoint on the public internet is the
# one thing that deployment must not create. With it off the service cannot run up an
# OpenAI bill: its sole upstream call is one embedding when someone presses Save.
# ENABLE_CHAT_ROUTES=true

# ---- Web app (Feature 5) ----
# Where the browser sends chat messages. It defaults to http://localhost:3001,
# so local development needs nothing set here; Feature 21 points it at the
# deployed API. NOTE: Create React App only reads REACT_APP_* variables from
# apps/web/.env or the shell environment — NOT from this root file. It is listed
# here so every knob is documented in one place.
# REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# ---- OpenAI (server-side only — never exposed to the browser) ----
# Used from Feature 4 onward. The key must live ONLY here, never in apps/web.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-your-openai-key-here
OPENAI_MODEL_REPLY=gpt-4o
OPENAI_MODEL_UTILITY=gpt-4o-mini
OPENAI_MODEL_EMBEDDING=text-embedding-3-small
# Override the OpenAI endpoint. Unset in normal use. Point it at an Azure/OpenAI-compatible
# gateway, or at a local stub to exercise the orchestrator without spending tokens — also
# how the "saved but not indexed" path is tested (aim it at a dead port).
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# Cap on reply length and how long to wait for OpenAI before giving up. The reply cap is a
# cost control first: output is billed at four times input on gpt-4o, so it is the most
# direct knob on what a turn costs. 600 tokens is roughly 400 words.
OPENAI_MAX_REPLY_TOKENS=600
OPENAI_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ---- Prompt assembly & cost controls (Feature 8) ----
# Model tiering is the biggest lever and it is already above: OPENAI_MODEL_UTILITY runs the
# match query and the running summary, OPENAI_MODEL_REPLY writes the counseling reply and
# nothing else. Pointing both at the same model is refused at startup — it would work
# perfectly and cost about seventeen times more, with nothing anywhere looking different.
#
# How many messages are replayed to the model word for word. Everything older is represented
# by a running summary instead, so a long conversation stays whole without the prompt growing
# with it. Counts messages, not exchanges: 20 is about ten turns each way.
# HISTORY_VERBATIM_MESSAGES=20
#
# How many messages must spill past that window before a summarization runs. Each one is an
# upstream call, so summarizing a batch at a time costs far less than summarizing on every
# turn past the window. The prompt never exceeds HISTORY_VERBATIM_MESSAGES + this, minus one.
# HISTORY_SUMMARY_BATCH=10
#
# Hard cap on the running summary. The prompt asks for at most 180 words; this is the stop.
# OPENAI_MAX_SUMMARY_TOKENS=400
#
# Prompt caching needs no configuration — OpenAI applies it automatically to a prompt prefix
# it has seen before, once that prefix passes 1024 tokens. What it needs is ORDER, which is
# enforced in services/api/src/prompt.ts: the static system prompt first, then the summary and
# the replayed turns, and only then the per-turn note carrying the language and the retrieved
# Care Pattern guidance. Watch `cachedPromptTokens` in the chat completion log line (or in the
# Feature 22 inspector) to confirm it is engaging.

# ---- Care Pattern retrieval / RAG (Feature 7) ----
# How close a student's situation must be to a Care Pattern before that pattern's guidance
# is put in front of them, as cosine similarity between 0 and 1. NOT a percentage and not
# portable: absolute values depend on the embedding model and on how the patterns are
# worded. The default is measured — `npm run retrieval:calibrate` scores labelled cases
# through the real pipeline and prints the band that separates correct matches from
# messages the library does not cover. Re-run it whenever the library changes materially.
# CARE_PATTERN_RELEVANCE_FLOOR=0.54
#
# At most this many patterns are blended into one reply.
# CARE_PATTERN_TOP_N=3
#
# How many of the student's own recent messages feed the match query. Only theirs: replaying
# our replies would feed injected guidance back into the query that selects the guidance.
# CARE_PATTERN_MATCH_WINDOW=4
#
# Below this many characters of student text, skip retrieval entirely. A cost guard rather
# than a relevance gate — "hi" cannot match anything and the floor would reject it anyway.
# CARE_PATTERN_MIN_SIGNAL_CHARS=12

# ---- Retrieval inspector on the chat page (Feature 22) ----
# Unlocks a panel on the chat page showing why a reply is what it is: the matched Care
# Patterns and their similarity scores, the relevance floor, the English match query, the
# guidance injected into the prompt, and — on request — the same message answered again
# with that guidance withheld, for a side-by-side comparison.
#
# UNSET MEANS THE INSPECTOR DOES NOT EXIST. No debug payload is built and the response is
# byte-identical to an ordinary visitor's, which is what a hosted instance should run with
# until someone deliberately turns it on. The credential grants visibility only: it cannot
# create, edit, publish or retire a Care Pattern, and it is deliberately NOT the Feature 17
# admin session, which can.
#
# Use a long random string. The browser sends it as the X-InnerSun-Inspect header; the
# comparison mode costs a second gpt-4o call per turn.
#
#   echo "INSPECTOR_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -base64 24)" >> .env
#
# CAREFUL: the placeholder below counts as UNSET. config.ts rejects the literal
# `choose-a-long-random-string` for the same reason it rejects `sk-your-openai-key-here` — a
# .env copied from this file and left unedited must not hand out a working credential. So
# uncommenting the line as-is switches nothing on. Put a real random value in.
#
# If the chat page shows "That reply came back with no inspector data", the token in the
# browser does not match the one this API started with — or nothing was set here at all.
# Note that the API reads this value ONCE, at startup: editing .env does nothing until the
# server is restarted, and a stale instance still holding port 3001 will keep answering with
# whatever token it was launched with (`lsof -nP -iTCP:3001 -sTCP:LISTEN` shows who owns it).
# INSPECTOR_TOKEN=choose-a-long-random-string

# ---- Booking nudge and the human hand-off (Feature 11) ----
# Where a student goes to book a real counselor: a scheduling link (Calendly, Cal.com, a
# university booking page). It belongs to the practice rather than to this codebase, so it
# is configuration and there is no default.
#
# UNSET MEANS THE NUDGE IS SWITCHED OFF. That is deliberate, and it is the same rule Feature 9
# applies to hotline numbers: nudging a student toward a counselor and then handing them
# nowhere to go is worse than not asking at all. With this unset the readiness check reports
# `disabled` on every turn, the startup log says so, and no booking card is ever attached to a
# reply. A malformed value is refused at startup rather than shipped — a broken link would be
# clicked at the exact moment a student decided to ask for help.
# BOOKING_URL=https://cal.com/your-practice/intro
#
# When the nudge fires. All rule-based, no model call, and all three numbers are CHOSEN
# rather than measured — what would measure them is conversion data, which arrives with
# Feature 19. See docs/PLAN.md Feature 11.
#
# Substantive student messages needed before turn count alone is enough:
# BOOKING_MIN_STUDENT_TURNS=6
# The lower bar when a matched Care Pattern carries escalation guidance — better evidence,
# so less of it is needed:
# BOOKING_ESCALATION_MIN_STUDENT_TURNS=3
# How many characters a message needs to count as substantive, so that six exchanges of
# "ok" / "thanks" never add up to readiness:
# BOOKING_MIN_TURN_CHARS=30
#
# A student who simply ASKS for a human ("can I talk to a real counselor?") is nudged
# immediately, whatever these say. That path needs no configuration.

# ---- Admin tool (Feature 17) ----
# Whether to serve POST /chat. On by default, because that endpoint is the product.
# Set to false on the HOSTED ADMIN instance, whose only job is letting researchers author
# Care Patterns: an open, unauthenticated, token-spending chat endpoint on the public
# internet is the one thing that deployment must not create. With it off, that service
# cannot run up an OpenAI bill — its sole upstream call is one embedding per save.
# Feature 21 turns it back on behind the abuse protection Feature 20 adds.
# ENABLE_CHAT_ROUTES=true
#
# Admin sessions need no secret: they are rows in `admin_sessions` keyed by the SHA-256 of
# a random cookie token, not a signed stateless cookie. Nothing to configure here.

# ---- Database: PostgreSQL + pgvector (added in Feature 3) ----
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/innersun

# The hosted database that holds the researcher-authored Care Patterns (Feature 17).
# Deliberately a SEPARATE variable from DATABASE_URL: the destructive scripts
# (`db:reset`, `db:seed`) read DATABASE_URL and refuse to run unless it points at
# localhost, so reaching production must never mean repointing that variable.
# Used only by `npm run db:pull:patterns` and `npm run db:export:patterns -- --remote`.
# REMOTE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@db.example.supabase.co:5432/postgres

# TLS is enabled automatically for any non-local database host, and the server's
# certificate IS verified.
#
# Supabase's Postgres presents a certificate chained to its OWN CA rather than a publicly
# trusted one, so a connection fails with "self-signed certificate in certificate chain"
# until that CA is supplied here. Download prod-ca-2021.crt from the Supabase dashboard
# (Database Settings -> SSL Configuration). This accepts either a file path or the
# certificate's PEM text — the text is what a hosting platform makes easy.
# DATABASE_SSL_CA=/path/to/prod-ca-2021.crt
#
# Last resort: keeps the connection encrypted but stops verifying who is on the other end,
# which anyone on the network path can exploit. Reach for DATABASE_SSL_CA first.
# DATABASE_SSL_NO_VERIFY=true
